general=[paper("Emergence of polarization in networks of large language model agents","Authors listed on publisher record",2026,"Nature Communications, 17, 11463","Nature Portfolio","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-78228-y","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-78228-y","methods","Experiments with interacting large-language-model agents show that decentralized agents can spontaneously form social networks with homophilic clustering and polarized opinion structure. The study analyzes how local interaction and network formation amplify separation even without an externally imposed polarized topology. Its social-science relevance lies in the controlled modeling of communication, homophily, contagion, and polarization, while the agents are simulations rather than human participants.","General top journal · social-science relevance",publicationDate="2026-10-14",publicationDateSource="Nature Communications article record",socialScienceReason="The research directly models communication networks, homophily, social influence, and polarization, although its empirical units are artificial agents rather than people.")]

